---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { Callout, StepHeading, DocCards } from '@/molecules/DocComponents/DocComponents';
---

<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Getting Started', href: '/docs/getting-started' }, { label: 'First Issue Walkthrough' }]}>
  <h1>First Issue Walkthrough</h1>

  <p>
    This walkthrough takes you from a fresh environment to your first merged pull request produced by
    the OpenFlows team. It is the fastest path to seeing the whole loop work.
  </p>

  <Callout type="info" title="Plan before code">
    OpenFlows will not let an agent start writing code before a plan is agreed. FORGE writes a plan and
    then halts; SENTINEL reviews that plan and either approves it or sends it back. Only an approved
    plan unlocks the next step. This gate is structural - skipping the review is not possible, even by
    accident.
  </Callout>

  <StepHeading step="1" id="stand-up">Stand up the control plane</StepHeading>
  <p>
    Start Manager, PostgreSQL, Redis, Coder, and the secret provider (see
    <a href="/docs/getting-started/installation">Installation</a>). Run Manager's setup and doctor checks,
    then confirm the public Manager URL and Coder URL are reachable.
  </p>

  <StepHeading step="2" id="bootstrap">Bootstrap the environment</StepHeading>
  <p>
    Run Manager setup and verify the deployment. This:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Checks the operator identity and Coder integration.</li>
    <li>Validates the template source and supported provisioner.</li>
    <li>Checks GitHub configuration, secret references, PostgreSQL, and Redis.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Bootstrap should complete cleanly. If it reports a missing integration - for example, GitHub
    authentication not set up - resolve that before continuing.
  </p>

  <StepHeading step="3" id="tenant">Add a tenant for your repository</StepHeading>
  <p>
    Sign into Manager, create or select an organization, install its GitHub App, and select an
    accessible repository. Create a tenant for that repository and wait for its Nexus provisioning
    operation and runtime heartbeat to succeed.
  </p>
  <p>
    A note on "tenant": in OpenFlows, one Coder server serves many teams. Each tenant is one team bound
    to one repository. If you only have one team, think of this simply as "setting up your project."
  </p>

  <StepHeading step="4" id="oauth">Verify the GitHub App connection</StepHeading>
  <p>
    Confirm the GitHub App installation can access the tenant repository. Runtimes request short-lived,
    repository-scoped installation tokens from Manager's broker. Hosted GitHub actions use the App bot
    identity. See <a href="/docs/getting-started/token-guide">Identity and tokens</a> for the full model.
  </p>

  <StepHeading step="5" id="notifications">(Optional) Turn on notifications</StepHeading>
  <p>
    For the smoothest experience, configure at least one notification channel (Slack, Discord, or
    WhatsApp). These are how OpenFlows tells you when it needs a human decision and how it surfaces
    reactions you must make. Without them, it still works - you would just check the control panel.
  </p>

  <StepHeading step="6" id="open-an-issue">Open an issue</StepHeading>
  <p>
    Create a clearly-scoped GitHub issue in the bound repository. The better the issue is described, the
    better the plan FORGE will produce. A good issue contains:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Context</strong> - why the change is needed.</li>
    <li><strong>Goal</strong> - a single sentence describing what success looks like.</li>
    <li><strong>Constraints</strong> - non-functional requirements such as performance or security.</li>
    <li><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong> - a checklist the reviewer can verify against.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>You do not need to write a plan, write tests, or assign the issue. The team handles all of that.</p>

  <Callout type="tip" title="What happens next">
    Watch the pipeline run:
    <ul>
      <li><strong>NEXUS</strong> picks the issue up and turns it into a ticket.</li>
      <li><strong>FORGE</strong> writes a plan and <strong>halts</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>SENTINEL</strong> reviews the plan and approves it.</li>
      <li><strong>FORGE</strong> implements, tests, and opens a pull request.</li>
      <li><strong>SENTINEL</strong> reviews the pull request adversarially.</li>
      <li><strong>VESSEL</strong> watches CI, resolves conflicts, and merges.</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>

  <StepHeading step="7" id="review">Review what came back</StepHeading>
  <p>
    When the work finishes, you get a merged pull request plus a record of the decisions made along the
    way. If at any point OpenFlows needed a human call - a security concern, an ambiguous spec, or a
    merge block - you were notified. Walk to that decision, resolve it, and the team continues. See
    <a href="/docs/getting-started/completion-and-merge">Completion & Merge</a> for how to review what
    came back.
  </p>

  <Callout type="tip" title="Continue reading">
    See <a href="/docs/getting-started/lifecycle-steps">Ticket Lifecycle</a> for the detailed
    phases every ticket travels through, and
    <a href="/docs/getting-started/completion-and-merge">Completion & Merge</a> for how the work wraps
    up.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
  <DocCards cards={[
    { icon: '🔁', title: 'Ticket Lifecycle', desc: 'The phases from issue detection through merge.', href: '/docs/getting-started/lifecycle-steps' },
    { icon: '✅', title: 'Completion & Merge', desc: 'How work is reviewed, merged, and torn down.', href: '/docs/getting-started/completion-and-merge' },
    { icon: '🔑', title: 'Identity and Tokens', desc: 'User login, runtime registration, and brokered GitHub access.', href: '/docs/getting-started/token-guide' },
  ]} />
</DocsLayout>
